  <h1 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 20px;">INTERVAL ANALYSIS: VERIFYING FEASIBILITY</h1>
  <h2 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Introduction.</h2>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Constrained optimization problems are of the form</p>
  <div style="margin-left: 100px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <p>minimize $\phi(\mathbf{x})$</p>
    <p>subject to $\mathbf{c}_i(\mathbf{x}) \le 0, i=1, \dots, m,$</p>
    <p>$\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x}) \le 0, j=1, \dots, q_1$</p>
    <p>$\mathbf{h}_k(\mathbf{x}) = 0, k=1, \dots, q_2$</p>
    <p>$\mathbf{x}_i \le \bar{x}_i, \mathbf{x}_i \ge \underline{x}_i, i=1, \dots, n.$</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">where $\phi: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}, \mathbf{c}_i: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}, \mathbf{g}_j: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}, \text{ and } \mathbf{h}_k: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}.$</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">(1)</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">In interval branch and bound algorithms for finding global optima for Problem (1), a search box of the form</p>
  <div style="margin-left: 100px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <p>$\mathbf{x} \in [\underline{x}_1, \bar{x}_1] \times [\underline{x}_2, \bar{x}_2] \times \dots \times [\underline{x}_n, \bar{x}_n]$</p>
    <p>$\underline{x}_i \le x_i \le \bar{x}_i, 1 \le i \le n.$</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">is generally given, where some of the sides in (2) correspond to bound constraints of Problem (1), and the other sides merely define the extent of the search region. If there are no constraints $\mathbf{c}_i$ and $\mathbf{g}_j$, then the box $\mathbf{x}$ is systematically tessellated into sub-boxes. The branch and bound algorithm, in its most basic form, proceeds as follows: Over each sub-box $\mathbf{x}_b$ ($\mathbf{x}_b$ is computed for some $b \in \mathbb{N}$ and the range of $\mathbf{x}$ over $b$ is bounded (e.g. with a straightforward interval evaluation). (See Interval analysis: Introduction, interval numbers and basic properties of interval arithmetic.) If there are no constraints $\mathbf{c}_i$ and $\mathbf{g}_j$, then the value $\phi(\mathbf{x})$ represents an upper bound on the minimum of $\phi$. The minimum such value is kept as the tessellation and search proceed; if any box $\mathbf{x}$ has a lower range bound greater than $\phi$, it is rejected as not containing a global optimum. See [1], [2], or [3] for details of such algorithms.</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Interval analysis: Introduction, interval numbers and basic properties of interval arithmetic. Interval analysis: Introduction, interval numbers and basic properties of interval arithmetic. Interval analysis: Interval Newton methods</p>
</div>

