  <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">possible normalization conditions. For details, see [1], §10.5] or [2, §2.5].</p>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">However, computational problems occur in practice with the system (3). It is more difficult to find a good approximate critical point (for an approximate small box $\mathbf{x}$) of the entire system (3) than it is to find a point where the inequality and equality constraints are satisfied. Furthermore, if an interval Newton method is applied to (3) over a large box, the corresponding interval Jacobi matrix or slope matrix typically contains singular matrices and hence is useless for existence verification. This is especially true if it is difficult to get good estimates for the Lagrange multipliers $\lambda_i$ and $\nu_j$. For this reason, the techniques outlined below are useful.</p>
  <h2 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Feasibility of inequality constraints.</h2>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">Proving feasibility of the inequality constraints is sometimes possible by evaluating the $\mathbf{g}_j$ with interval arithmetic. If $\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x}) \le 0$, then every point in $\mathbf{x}$ is feasible with respect to the constraint $\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x}) \le 0$. See Interval analysis: Introduction, interval numbers, and basic properties of interval arithmetic [3]. However, if $\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x})$ is active, then $\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x})$ and no conclusion can be reached from an interval evaluation. In such cases, feasibility can sometimes be proven by treating $\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x}) = 0$ as one of the equality constraints, using the techniques below.</p>
  <h2 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Infeasibility.</h2>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">An inequality constraint $\mathbf{g}_j$ is proven infeasible over $\mathbf{x}$ if $\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x}) > 0$ and an equality constraint $\mathbf{c}_i$ is infeasible over $\mathbf{x}$ if either $\mathbf{c}_i(\mathbf{x}) > 0$ or $\mathbf{c}_i(\mathbf{x}) \le 0$. See Interval analysis: Introduction, interval numbers, and basic properties of interval arithmetic [3].</p>
  <h2 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 20px;">Feasibility of equality constraints.</h2>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">The equality constraints</p>
  <div style="margin-left: 100px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <p>$\mathbf{c}_i(\mathbf{x}) = 0, \quad \mathbf{c}_i(\mathbf{x})^\circ = 0,$</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">$\mathbf{c}: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^m, \mathbf{c}_i \in \mathbb{R}^n$ can be considered an underdetermined system of equations, whereas interval Newton methods generally prove existence / uniqueness for square systems. However,</p>
