    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The situation is more complicated in the con- strained case. In particular, the values $\phi(\mathbf{x})$ can not be taken as upper bounds on the global optimum unless it is known that $\mathbf{x}$ is feasible. More generally, an upper bound on the range of $\phi$ over a small box $\mathbf{x}$ can be taken as an up- per bound for the global optimum provided it is proven that there exists a feasible point of Prob- lem (1) within $\mathbf{x}$. This article outlines how this can be done.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">For the fundamental concepts used through- out this article, see Interval analysis: Intro- duction, interval numbers and basic prop- erties of interval arithmetic.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">General feasibility: the Fritz-John condi- tions.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">An interval Newton method (see Interval analysis: Interval Newton methods) can sometimes be used to prove existence of a feasi- ble point of Problem (1) that is a critical point of $\phi$. In particular, the interval Newton method can sometimes prove existence of a solution to the Lagrange multiplier or Fritz-John system within $\mathbf{x}$. For the Fritz-John system, it is con- venient to consider the $\mathbf{g}_j$ bound constraints in the same form as the $\mathbf{g}_j$ inequality con- straints, so that there are $\mathbf{g}_j = \mathbf{q}_1 + \mathbf{g}_j$ general in- equality constraints of the form $\mathbf{g}_j(\mathbf{x}) \le 0$. With that, the Fritz-John system can be written as</p>
      <div style="margin-left: 100px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <p>$\left(\begin{array}{c} \mathbf{u}_0 \nabla \phi(\mathbf{x}) + \sum_{j=1}^{q_1} \mathbf{u}_j \nabla \mathbf{g}_j + \sum_{k=1}^{q_2} \mathbf{v}_k \nabla \mathbf{h}_k(\mathbf{x}) \\ \mathbf{g}_j \\ \mathbf{h}_k \end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{c} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{array}\right)$</p>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">(2)</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Certain complications arise. For example, if bound constraints or inequality constraints are active, then either the point $\mathbf{x}$ must be per- turbed or else the bound or inequality con- straints must be treated as equality constraints. Handling this case by perturbation is discussed in [2, p. 191].</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">For the original explanation of the Gaussian elimination-based process, see [1, §12.4]. In [2, §5.2.4], additional background, discussion, and references appear.</p>
    </div>
